                identify various semantically important chunks in documents, automatically label them with
                appropriate datatypes and semantic roles, and use this enhanced information to assist authors
                and to support downstream processes. Chunk locations, datatypes, and semantic roles can often
                be automatically determined from what is here called ``context'', to wit, the combination of
                their formatting, structure, and content; those of adjacent or nearby content; overall patterns
                of occurrence in a document, and similarities of all these things across documents (mainly but
                not exclusively among documents in the same document set). Similarity is not limited to exact
                or fuzzy string or property comparisons, but may include similarity of natural language
